  "body": "## What problem are you trying to solve?\n\nFiltering `<select>` menus is a common UI/UX pattern on the web today and has become an expectation both for users and developers, especially for lists that contain dozens or hundreds of options. Users often want to quickly type and narrow down results. While many JavaScript libraries provide this behavior, often by building custom “combobox” components that simulate a text input tied to a dropdown, these solutions typically require replacing the native `<select>` element entirely.\n\nThere’s currently no built-in or semantic way in HTML to connect a plain `<input>` to a `<select>` for filtering purposes. For many use cases, developers don’t want to fully re-implement a combobox; they want a lightweight, accessible way to make native `<select>` elements searchable, without sacrificing built-in keyboard support, form integration, or semantics.\n\n## What solutions exist today?\n\nThere are many JavaScript-based solutions that simulate filterable selects, but they come with tradeoffs. Most of them:\n\n* Replace the native `<select>` entirely with a custom component\n* Lose built-in accessibility and keyboard support\n* Add a significant amount of complexity for a relatively small feature\n* Require developers to write or copy filtering logic even when they just want something simple\n\nThere is no native HTML mechanism to declaratively link a text input to a select for filtering purposes.\n\n## How would you solve it?\n\nI propose two complementary enhancements to HTML that would allow web-native, accessible filtering of `<select>` elements using `<input>` elements:\n1. Using `for` to associate an external `<input>` with a `<select>`\n2. Allowing an `<input>` inside a `<select>` to act as a built-in filter\n\n<table>\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>\n        Option 1<br>\n        <code>&lt;input for=\"x\" /&gt; &lt;select id=\"x\"&gt;...</code>\n      </th>\n      <th>\n        Option 2<br>\n        <code>&lt;select&gt; &lt;input /&gt; &lt;/select&gt;</code>\n      </th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td width=\"350\" valign=\"top\" align=\"center\"><br><img width=\"280\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/77529c25-0cf8-4a84-a697-1429c3426caf\" alt=\"using for attribute to associate an external input element with a select element\"><br><small><br><code>&lt;input&gt;</code> exists outside the <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> and can be anywhere on the page or scoped by a <code>&lt;form&gt;</code></small><br>&nbsp;</td>\n      <td width=\"350\" valign=\"top\" align=\"center\"><br><img width=\"280\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/7141f7e5-55fe-4a92-9dfc-0406da085d1a\" alt=\"allowing an input element inside a select element to act as a built-in filter\"><br><small><br><code>&lt;input&gt;</code> exists inside the <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> and visually replaces the <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> element's button</small><br>&nbsp;</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\nBoth could be implemented and supported simultaneously:\n\n### Option 1: Using `for` to associate an external `<input>` with a `<select>`\n\nAllow `<input>` elements to use the `for` attribute to link to one or more `<select>` elements for the purpose of filtering options by text.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```html\n<input type=\"text\" for=\"fruit-select\" placeholder=\"Type to filter\">\n<select id=\"fruit-select\">\n  <option>Apple</option>\n  <option>Banana</option>\n  <option>Cherry</option>\n  <option>Date</option>\n</select>\n```\n\nWhen the user types in the input, only options in the associated select that match the input text would remain visible. The filter could match case-insensitively by default and support substring matching. The unfiltered options would still remain in the DOM but be hidden visually.\n\nThis would preserve keyboard accessibility and native `<select>` behavior while giving developers a way to enhance long dropdowns in a declarative way.\n\nThe `for` attribute already exists to associate form controls, and it fits well here as a mechanism to link an input to the select it controls. This could be extended to support multiple selects using space-separated IDs.\n\n### Option 2: Allowing an `<input>` inside a `<select>` to act as a built-in filter\n\nIn addition to the above, placing an `<input>` element directly inside a `<select>` could indicate that the input should act as a native filter for that select’s options.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```html\n<select>\n  <input placeholder=\"Filter options...\">\n  <option>Apple</option>\n  <option>Banana</option>\n  <option>Cherry</option>\n  <option>Date</option>\n</select>\n```\n\nWhen the input is typed into, the select options would be filtered based on the input text using the same behavior as described above. This approach would be useful in self-contained patterns, where the filtering UI and the list are tightly coupled within a single component structure.\n\nBoth of these enhancements would work in parallel and give developers flexible ways to enable native filtering with minimal effort, while still preserving semantics and accessibility.\n\n## Anything else?\n\n* Filtering behavior could be customizable later (e.g. with attributes like `filtermode=\"starts-with inclusive\"`)\n* It would be important to ensure accessibility, especially for screen reader users and keyboard navigation\n* Filtering could work without JavaScript, making it more robust in progressive enhancement scenarios\n* This could be a small but meaningful quality-of-life improvement for forms with large or dynamic selects",
